<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Senlis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Augustomagus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Senlis is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. As Augustomagus it was the chief town of the Gallic Silvanectes under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and its late Roman walls partly survive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a> was elected king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> there in 987, and its early Gothic cathedral was begun in 1153.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/senlis.jpg" alt="Senlis" width="320" height="303" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tango7174 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Senlis_NDame1_tango7174.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Senlis stands on the river Nonette, 40 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, between the forests of Chantilly and Ermenonville to the south and Halatte to the north. The highest point of the town, 140 metres, lies in the heart of the forest of Halatte, and the lowest on the banks of the Nonette. The area is a vast limestone plateau of the Lutetian, covered mostly in silt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was known in early imperial times as Augustomagus and later as Civitas Silvanectium, the city of the Silvanectes. In the 3rd century a defensive wall seven metres high was built round it in response to Frankish incursions; about half of it still stands, and it remained in use into the 13th century. A Roman amphitheatre seating as many as 10,000 people stood about 500 m west of the walled town, and its remains are still visible. Monarchs of the early French dynasties lived at Senlis, drawn by the venison of the Chantilly forest, and built a castle on the foundations of the Roman settlement.</p><p>Senlis came into the ownership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a> in 981. In 987 Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adalbero-of-reims">Adalbero of Reims</a> called an assembly and asked it to choose Hugh as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>; he was crowned at Noyon. Under Capetian rule Senlis became a royal city and remained one until the reign of Charles X.</p><p>It reached its height in the 12th and 13th centuries as trade in wool and leather increased and vineyards were planted; a second set of ramparts, larger and higher than the Gallo-Roman ones, was built under Philip II, and King Louis VII granted a municipal charter in 1173. The Bailiwick of Senlis was created in 1265. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> devastated the town, though it escaped destruction despite a siege by the Armagnacs, and in 1493 Charles VIII signed the Treaty of Senlis there with Maximilian I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. In early September 1914 the Germans occupied the town and executed its mayor, Eugène Odent, with six other civilian hostages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The cathedral, a Gothic church and national monument, was built between 1153 and 1519; its spire dates from the 13th century, and the southern portal of the 16th century is the work of Martin Chambiges. The former bishopric was abolished by the Concordat of 1801. The royal castle, whose present building dates from the 12th century under Louis VII, stands beside the priory of Saint Maurice. St Vincent Abbey was founded in 1065 by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-of-kiev">Anne of Kiev</a>. The town has museums of art and archaeology, of hunting and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spahis">Spahis</a>, and its old streets have often served as a film setting.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Senlis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Rome they formed a civitas with <b>Augustomagus</b> (Senlis) as its chief town.</blockquote><a href="/state/silvanectes">Silvanectes</a></li></ul></section>
